user_prompt:
Variables are listed below in random order. Use the exact id strings.
[
  {
    "id": "Dyspnoea",
    "description": "dyspnoea",
    "states": "True|False",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "Xray",
    "description": "getting positive xray result",
    "states": "positive|negative",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "Cancer",
    "description": "cancer",
    "states": "True|False",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "Smoker",
    "description": "smoking habit",
    "states": "True|False",
    "state_count": "2"
  },
  {
    "id": "Pollution",
    "description": "exposure to pollutants",
    "states": "low|high",
    "state_count": "2"
  }
]

Task: propose a causal order prior. Earlier variables are potential causes or upstream conditions; later variables are downstream effects, measurements, symptoms, decisions, or aggregate outcomes.

Return one JSON object with this schema:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["exact id 1", "exact id 2", "..."],
  "confidence": 0.0,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {"before": "exact id", "after": "exact id", "confidence": 0.0}
  ],
  "rationale": "brief, no more than 80 words"
}

Constraints: ordered_nodes must contain every provided id exactly once; do not invent nodes; if evidence is weak, still provide the best semantic prior and lower the confidence.

raw_response:
{
  "ordered_nodes": ["Pollution", "Smoker", "Cancer", "Xray", "Dyspnoea"],
  "confidence": 0.87,
  "high_confidence_pairs": [
    {
      "before": "Pollution",
      "after": "Cancer",
      "confidence": 0.78
    },
    {
      "before": "Smoker",
      "after": "Cancer",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "before": "Cancer",
      "after": "Xray",
      "confidence": 0.86
    },
    {
      "before": "Cancer",
      "after": "Dyspnoea",
      "confidence": 0.82
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Pollution and smoking are upstream risk factors, cancer is the disease outcome, and xray/dyspnoea are downstream manifestations or measurements of disease. Exact ordering between pollution and smoking is less certain, so they are placed first without implying causality between them."
}
